  computingOverviewArticle.sections.push({
    id:"programming",title:"Software and programming cultures",html:"<p>The world's software divides into five durable cultures rather than one linear succession of languages. Germany's <a href='rechenplan.html'>Rechenplan</a> and <a href='planform.html'>Planform</a> make programs certified plans; the <a href='commonwealth-programming-languages.html'>Commonwealth tradition</a> treats computing as a documented information service; <a href='american-programming-languages.html'>American languages</a> emphasize replaceable tools and competing platforms; <a href='japanese-programming-languages.html'>Japanese languages</a> formalize controlled procedures in native vertical source; and <a href='latin-programming-languages.html'>Latin-bloc languages</a> emphasize intelligible instruments and multilingual exchange.</p><p>See <a href='programming-languages.html'>Programming languages</a> for the comparative history and <a href='software-portability.html'>Software portability</a> for the distinction among algorithmic, source, intermediate-object, and binary compatibility.</p>"
  });
